  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 334 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nThere is very good agreement amongst both ensemble and deterministic \nguidance that the upper level low approaching the region will become \ncut-off as it slowly arrives into Florida Thursday night. This setup \nwill create much more unsettled weather across South Florida for \nFriday and this weekend, with PoPs climbing above 80% each day \nfrom Friday through the weekend. \n\nContinued deep moisture (with PWATs above 2 inches) and increased \nforcing aloft from the upper level low should allow for scattered to \nnumerous storms to develop across the region. While there is still \nsome spread in the guidance, most ensemble QPF clusters are only \nshowing about 1.5 to 2.5 inches of rainfall through Saturday evening \nperiod. These amounts shouldn't result in anything more than a few \nisolated urban flooding impacts for low lying and poor drainage \nlocations. With the upper low moving overhead for Friday and into \nthe weekend, increased forcing may allow for a few training storms \nto hit the same area multiple times. As the low crosses into the \nGulf over the weekend, there is also an indication that there could \nbe some coastal convergence and pinned coastal storms. While not \nquite perpendicular to each other, winds at 500 and 700 mbs will be \nout of the SW, while winds at the surface will be out of the E to \nNE. This may lead to slow moving storms and enhanced moisture \nconvergence along the Paradise Coast, which also happens to be where \nmost ensemble QPF clusters show the highest rainfall amounts to be \n(although mostly offshore). \n\nWhile more uncertain, there will also be an isolated threat of \nsevere weather as the disturbance crosses the region. Some models \nare showing that 500 mb temperatures may drop into the -8 to -9 C \nrange, within the 25th percentile for this time of year. This could \nresult in some steeper lapse rates, allowing for some stronger cores \nto develop and for a low chance of hail and strong downburst winds. \nHowever, increased cloud cover may inhibit the amount of diurnal \nheating and instability that accumulates throughout the day, keeping \nthe threat of strong storms more conditional and low confidence. \n\nAs the upper level low shifts offshore into the Gulf late this \nweekend and into early next week, low level flow will shift more \nsoutherly. This should allow for fairly uniform rain chances across \nSoFlo (~80-85%) as deep moisture will remain present across the \nregion. High temperatures will remain in the upper 80s to low 90s \nacross SoFlo, with lows in the 70s. Once the upper low ejects out to \nthe west on Sunday, we should return to our more typical sea-breeze \ndominated wind regime, with scattered afternoon storms.",
